-0.01264190673828125, 0.02276611328125, 0.032989501953125, -0.0025997161865234375, -0.0113525390625, 0.05523681640625, 0.0058746337890625, 0.03814697265625, -0.035888671875, -0.005992889404296875, -0.026214599609375, 0.00472259521484375, 0.00872802734375, -0.03814697265625, 0.0033283233642578125, -0.032562255859375, 0.00482177734375, -0.01343536376953125, 0.0058441162109375, -0.001445770263671875, -0.043548583984375, -0.0026226043701171875, -0.00251007080078125, -0.0045318603515625, -0.009368896484375, 0.0244140625, 0.0013284683227539062, 0.015777587890625, -0.0032901763916015625, -0.0153045654296875, 0.001094818115234375, 0.0024166107177734375, -0.00853729248046875, -0.025726318359375, 0.020416259765625, 0.0509033203125, 0.019805908203125, 0.001743316650390625, -0.001953125, -0.0201416015625, 0.0274658203125, 0.00826263427734375, -0.0004930496215820312, -0.018157958984375, 0.0305938720703125, 0.0030765533447265625, -0.023773193359375, -0.003437042236328125, -0.00797271728515625, 0.00878143310546875, 0.01517486572265625, 0.0161285400390625, -0.005481719970703125, -0.01439666748046875, 0.005908966064453125, -0.00110626220703125, -0.0012683868408203125, -0.00730133056640625, 0.006282806396484375, 0.0018949508666992188, -0.02886962890625, -0.002437591552734375, 0.026824951171875, 0.00318145751953125, 0.0183868408203125, 0.05255126953125, -0.031341552734375, 0.0261383056640625, -0.003536224365234375, -0.01617431640625, 0.00904083251953125, 0.02398681640625, -0.022491455078125, -0.004642486572265625, 0.01166534423828125, 0.008544921875, 0.00798797607421875, 0.0031833648681640625, -0.00518035888671875, 0.01557159423828125, -0.02630615234375, 0.0285491943359375, 0.004085540771484375, 0.006137847900390625, 0.058624267578125, 0.00510406494140625, -0.0157318115234375, -0.0163421630859375, 0.04510498046875, -0.0131988525390625, 0.01448822021484375, -0.00492095947265625, 0.039459228515625, -0.0098876953125, 0.0151519775390625, -0.02105712890625, 0.018951416015625, 0.0194244384765625, 0.0248260498046875, -0.0081787109375, -0.017913818359375, -0.004039764404296875, 0.024688720703125, 0.0064239501953125, -0.01114654541015625, 0.046905517578125, -0.0245513916015625, 0.013031005859375, 0.008087158203125, 0.0079193115234375, 0.044708251953125, 0.028228759765625, 0.0191650390625, -0.01456451416015625, 0.033416748046875, -0.003559112548828125, 0.01165008544921875, -0.006160736083984375, 0.0126953125, -0.020233154296875, -0.02423095703125, 0.002765655517578125, 0.0038166046142578125, -0.0144195556640625, 0.0114288330078125, 0.044586181640625, -0.01267242431640625, -0.0150146484375, -0.032958984375, 0.0183563232421875, -0.0106201171875, -0.01032257080078125, 0.0303497314453125, 0.00543975830078125, 0.001918792724609375, -0.0207061767578125, -0.0102081298828125, 0.003910064697265625, -0.01074981689453125, -0.034576416015625, 0.011993408203125, -0.02191162109375, 0.01134490966796875, -0.0117950439453125, -0.00955963134765625, 0.0194091796875, -0.00035858154296875, 0.0007610321044921875, -0.01264190673828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines\u2014such as those underlying LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022)\u2014optimize individual, task-specific datasets in isolation. This restricts systematic transfer of supervision across related generative tasks, hinders efficient organization of multimodal data, and complicates unified progress. A key unresolved challenge is how to curate and organize datasets to enable cross-capability transfer while faithfully capturing dependencies among generative skills.\n\n---\n\n**Background and Motivation:**  \nRecent successes in multimodal generation have been shaped by large web-scale datasets and annotation pipelines. Yet, current approaches treat tasks like text-to-image synthesis, image editing, and entity association independently, missing opportunities for shared supervision and efficient training.\n\n---\n\n**Our contributions:**  \n1. **Unified capability-driven data infrastructure:** We introduce a data framework defining and aligning three foundational generative capabilities: (a) text-image grounding (linking texts to semantically-matched images), (b) inter-image transformation (trackable image editing), and (c) image-knowledge association (connecting images to factual or ontological entities).\n2. **Curriculum scheduling exploiting task dependencies:** This includes building and validating a capability dependency graph to order training tasks optimally for transfer, using empirical evaluations.\n3. **Technical advances:** We present methods for large-scale data filtering, annotation, and bias mitigation, featuring granular QA protocols and measurable demographic rebalancing.\n4. **Comprehensive empirical evaluation:** Our ablations and error analysis measure the impact of each data engine, curriculum strategy, and bias protocol on multimodal generation benchmarks.\n\n---\n\n**Definitions and Dataset Structure:**  \nA **generative capability** is a distinct, functional skill a model acquires. We delineate capabilities as follows:\n- **Text-image grounding:** Ability to align textual descriptions with images (e.g., pairing \"a red fire truck\" with matching photographs); annotation schemas enforce this by requiring explicit text-image semantic matching, excluding any knowledge or editing metadata.\n- **Inter-image transformation:** Ability to modify images according to specified edits (e.g., changing \"daytime cityscape\" into \"nighttime\"); supervision uses before/after pairs labeled with explicit, human-verifiable transformation tags (e.g., \"sky color change: blue\u2192black\"), ensuring edits are isolated from general text-image or entity information.\n- **Image-knowledge association:** Ability to connect images to structured knowledge entities (e.g., linking a cityscape to \"Bruges Belfry (Q2364)\" in Wikidata); annotation restricts supervision to unambiguous entity IDs, disallowing open-ended text or edits.\n\nCapabilities are kept separate in dataset schemas by:\n- Using non-overlapping annotation fields for each task,\n- Cross-validating to prevent identical images from leaking between capabilities unless intentionally paired, and\n- Conducting double-blind batch annotation for ambiguous cases.\n\nFigure 1 (appendix) illustrates the three engines and scheduler. Briefly:  \n- **Text-Image Grounding Engine:** Collects and filters text-image pairs from web, internal, and crowdsourced sources. Captioning uses ensemble models and domain-expert annotators. For quality control, >5% of annotation batches undergo double-blind review, keeping Cohen's \u03ba > 0.89.\n- **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:** Aggregates editing data from benchmarks (e.g., CelebA-HQ Edits), algorithmic augmentations, and curated user edits. Edit saliency is checked by classifiers (ResNet-50, ROC-AUC 94%), and random audits clear >90% recognizable edits.\n- **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:** Employs linkage tools (BLINK/TagMe) with structured corpora to assign precise entity labels, resolved via majority vote among five annotators for ambiguous cases.\n\nSummaries of annotation workflows, data sources, and frequency statistics appear in Table 1. For instance, the final dataset compiles 440M captioned T2I pairs, 120M editing pairs, and 27M image-entity pairs, all filtered for inappropriate, low-confidence, or duplicate content (13% discarded overall). Final aggregate annotation agreement rates span \u03ba = 0.85\u20130.91.\n\n---\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Task Dependencies:**  \nA **capability-aligned curriculum scheduler** sequences model training by exploiting empirically measured dependencies among tasks. We:\n- Hold out each target capability (e.g., fine-grained entity retrieval) and train variants on only foundational capabilities (e.g., text-image grounding).\n- Downstream tasks include compositional image synthesis, multi-step editing, and entity retrieval.\n- Metrics: zero-shot retrieval accuracy, Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID, using 50k sample protocol), targeted edit classification accuracy, and entity-level micro-precision. All are computed on equal-sized validation splits (10M text-image, 10M edit, 2M entity pairs).\n- The dependency graph is built by noting which upstream skills most boost each held-out target (e.g., text-image grounding boosts editing accuracy by 3.5% absolute, from 59.6% baseline to 63.1%).\n- Greedy curriculum search and ablation identify optimal sequencing, dynamically increasing frequencies if transfer plateaus or validation error spikes occur.\n\n---\n\n**Bias Mitigation Protocols:**  \nTo address demographic and content bias, we deploy:\n1. **Subgroup Definition and Coverage:** Adopting FairFace's race/ethnicity and gender classes and Balanced ImageNet object categories. Each batch is checked for subgroup coverage (>5% representation, or at least the median).\n2. **Thresholds and Rebalancing:** Subgroups falling below thresholds are upsampled; deviations >2 SD trigger synthetic augmentation. We track unique entity ratio, class entropy, and edit diversity (mean entropy increase from 0.65 to 0.79 after rebalancing).\n3. **Synthetic Augmentation:** For rare subgroups, we use GAN-based synthesis (StyleGAN2), attribute swapping, and compositional image blending. Bias audits (10k sample per image class) show underrepresented group accuracy improved from 79.2% (\u00b10.8%) to 87.1% (\u00b10.6%), with F1 score increases up to 8 points.\n\n---\n\n**Annotation Quality Assurance:**  \nCaption experts are selected from verified crowdsourcing platforms, passing:\n- Stage 1: Language reasoning exam (\u226598%, 30 Q).\n- Stage 2: Task-specific gold-standard annotation (\u226595%, 50 items).\nCalibration, confusion item training, and >5% spot monthly double-blind checks maintain consistency (overall mean error rate 6.7%, mostly due to lexical ambiguity). Full statistics per annotator are shown in Table 2.\n\n---\n\n**Empirical Evaluation and Results:**  \nWe ablate each core method:\n- **Curriculum scheduling:** Disabling it drops CLIP retrieval accuracy from 80.6% to 77.1% on test splits (\u00b10.3% SD, n=3 runs).\n- **Inter-Image Transformation:** Removing editing data reduces edit classification accuracy from 63.1% to 55.8% (\u0394=7.3%, p<0.01 over 3 runs, paired t-test).\n- **Bias rebalancing:** Omitting it reduces subgroup F1 score for underrepresented groups from 82.5% to 74.5%.\nComplete results for our 3B and 6B diffusion models, and LAION-5B baselines are detailed in Table 3 (e.g., FID@50k val/test: ours 7.34/6.92 vs. baseline 8.21/7.95).\n\nQualitative results (see Figure 2, appendix) show generalization from complex prompts to consistent edits (e.g., \"a red truck in heavy snow\" \u2192 \"remove snow\"), but errors persist for rare composite entities (e.g., \"two persons in traditional Sami dress\"), fine attribute swaps, and rare (<0.1%) entity knowledge association, where micro-precision is up to 15% below common classes.\n\n---\n\n**Limitations:**  \n(1) **Long-tail entity coverage:** Our largest models underperform by up to 12% (absolute micro-precision, 58.5% vs. 70.5%) on the rarest 5% of entities, due to insufficient data.  \n(2) **Residual demographic and attribute biases:** Despite protocols, stereotype leakage (e.g., cultural attire, gendered object association) persists, especially when training on web-sourced imagery.\n(3) **Scalability and curation bottlenecks:** Manual annotation and verification requirements may impede scaling beyond current size or to new domains, posing ongoing challenges for both quality and cost.\n(4) **Evaluation limits:** Some metrics (e.g., FID, CLIP accuracy) do not capture semantic fidelity or subjective quality, and error rates in fine-grained capabilities remain above target thresholds.\n\nFuture work includes extending annotation ontologies for rare entities, improving synthetic augmentation realism, and developing more robust, interpretable evaluation metrics for multimodal generative models."}}